      "copy": {
        "a_1": "QBL",
        "nav_1": " / ",
        "a_2": "Growing & Gathering",
        "nav_2": " / ",
        "span_1": "Lettuce companions",
        "span_2": "Growing & gathering / The gentle company",
        "h1_1": "What to grow",
        "h1_2": "near ",
        "em_1": "lettuce.",
        "p_1": "Keep it cool.",
        "p_2": "Keep it reachable.",
        "p_3": "Keep the neighbors gentle.",
        "p_4": "Carrots, radishes, scallions, chives and a little dill make a useful starting list. Leave the big summer sprawl out of the lettuce’s way.",
        "a_3": "Find its neighbors ",
        "span_3": "↗",
        "figcaption_1": "Reference lettuce photography · Néstor Santos / Pexels",
        "a_4": "Good company ",
        "span_4": "↓",
        "a_5": "Picture the patch ",
        "span_5": "↓",
        "a_6": "Look for trouble ",
        "span_6": "↓",
        "a_7": "Our garden ",
        "span_7": "↓",
        "span_8": "A little variety / A calm bed",
        "h2_1": "Let lettuce stay",
        "em_2": "the main crop.",
        "p_5": "Think narrow, quick or nearby. A companion earns its place by fitting the bed—not by appearing on a magic chart.",
        "span_9": "The companion shelf",
        "h3_1": "Radishes",
        "p_6": "A quick early crop between slower spots. Give both crops their room.",
        "span_10": "Take a look ",
        "span_11": "↗",
        "span_12": "The companion shelf",
        "h3_2": "Carrots",
        "p_7": "A nearby strip, rather than a crowded layer under the lettuce.",
        "span_13": "Take a look ",
        "span_14": "↗",
        "span_15": "The companion shelf",
        "h3_3": "Chives at the edge",
        "p_8": "A narrow edible edge, with room for the lettuce. Scallions are another option in this plan.",
        "span_16": "Take a look ",
        "span_17": "↗",
        "strong_1": "And a little dill?",
        "p_9": "A little dill can fit. Keep it from becoming a wall of foliage. Afternoon relief in heat is different from lettuce being smothered by a bigger crop.",
        "span_18": "Show me / A reachable patch",
        "h2_2": "A small salad garden.",
        "em_3": "Not a leafy traffic jam.",
        "div_1": "Scallions or chives · narrow edge",
        "div_2": "Carrots in their own strip",
        "div_3": "Early radish pockets",
        "div_4": "Lettuce · keep the harvest side reachable",
        "p_10": "Arrangement only; not to scale. No plant counts, exact spacing or bed dimensions are implied.",
        "h3_4": "Cooler timing.",
        "p_11": "Lettuce is a cool-season crop. Heat and a dry bed can make the plan harder to manage.",
        "h3_5": "Steady moisture.",
        "p_12": "Avoid the swing between soggy and dried out. Keep checking conditions around the roots.",
        "h3_6": "Small repeats.",
        "p_13": "Try small repeated plantings rather than one large all-at-once patch. Match timing to your conditions.",
        "a_8": "Plan your own mix ",
        "span_19": "↗",
        "span_20": "Look low / Look closely",
        "h2_3": "Tender leaves show",
        "em_4": "trouble quickly.",
        "p_14": "Check folds, leaf undersides and the soil surface. A hole or curled leaf is a clue, not a diagnosis.",
        "span_21": "Reference comparison / Slug",
        "h3_7": "Holes or slime trails?",
        "p_15": "Compare what is actually in the bed. Slugs are one possibility; inspect low hiding places and the soil surface.",
        "span_22": "Take a look ",
        "span_23": "↗",
        "span_24": "Reference comparison / Aphids",
        "h3_8": "Tiny insects in the folds?",
        "p_16": "Look for the insects themselves and sticky residue. Confirm identification before choosing a response.",
        "span_25": "Take a look ",
        "span_26": "↗",
        "summary_1": "Other clues worth checking",
        "p_17": "Young plants damaged at soil level can point toward cutworms. Pale winding leaf tunnels can suggest leafminers; earwigs are another possibility discussed in the source. Compare the actual damage and insect rather than treating every chewed leaf alike.",
        "h3_9": "Don’t crowd it, then forget it.",
        "p_18": "Big summer plants, hard drying between waterings and damp hiding spots can all make the bed harder to manage. Review damage early; don’t assume a homemade spray is harmless.",
        "figcaption_2": "From Jeff & Vanessa’s actual backyard journal.",
        "span_27": "Our place / The continuing story",
        "h2_4": "Our garden began",
        "em_5": "with one row.",
        "p_19": "The cabin’s first trial row came with fencing work, a water pump and leaks to chase. It is the beginning of our garden story—not a documented trial of every lettuce companion.",
        "a_9": "Read our garden story ",
